<!--
  THE BACK AFFORDANCE'S GLYPH. @drawable/ic_chevron_right mirrored about x=12, drawn
  rather than flipped at runtime.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Both headers used to carry ic_chevron_right under
  android:scaleX="-1", and that mirror does not survive: every icon button in the
  product wears @anim/press_icon (via @style/Widget.Departament.Button.Icon), whose
  DEFAULT item — the resting one, not the pressed one — animates scaleX to 1.0. The
  state list animator fires the moment the view attaches, so the arrow flipped itself
  back to pointing RIGHT before the user ever saw it, on all 17 sub-pages plus every
  screen on @layout/view_toolbar. An arrow's direction is the entire message a back
  control carries, so this was never cosmetic.

  autoMirrored, not scaleX: in RTL a back control points the other way, and the
  platform does that here — in the drawable, where TalkBack, the press animator and
  the touch target all stay untouched. A negative scaleX on the VIEW mirrors the
  container too, which moves the glyph inside its own target and fights RTL.

  The path is ic_chevron_right's with every x replaced by 24 − x, so the two glyphs
  are the same stroke weight, the same 8.8×14.8 ink and the same optical mass:
      right  M8.6,4.6 L7.2,6 l6,6 l-6,6 l1.4,1.4 L16,12 z    ink x 7.2 … 16
      left   M15.4,4.6 L16.8,6 l-6,6 l6,6 l-1.4,1.4 L8,12 z  ink x 8 … 16.8
  That ink matches the prototype's back glyph exactly (docs/design2026/port-shots-2/
  40-perapp.geo.json: path w 8.8, h 14.8, 8dp inside its 24dp box).
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp" android:height="24dp" android:viewportWidth="24" android:viewportHeight="24"
    android:autoMirrored="true"
    android:tint="?attr/colorControlNormal">
    <path android:fillColor="@android:color/white" android:pathData="M15.4,4.6L16.8,6l-6,6l6,6l-1.4,1.4L8,12z" />
</vector>
